-- title:
--   Frobenius twist shifts the kernel ideal by P
-- statement:
--   Let $\kappa$ be an algebraically closed field with decidable equality, let $q'$ be prime, and assume $\kappa$ has characteristic $q'$ and is algebraic over $\mathbb{Z}/q'$. Let $X_1/\kappa$ be an elliptic Weierstrass curve with no nonzero $q'$-torsion point ($q'\cdot P=0\Rightarrow P=0$), and let $a,b\in\mathbb{Q}$ satisfy `IsDefiniteRamifiedExactlyAt`: $a<0$, $b<0$, and for each height-one prime $v$ of $\mathcal{O}_{\mathbb{Q}}$ the algebra $\mathbb{H}[\mathbb{Q},a,b]\otimes_{\mathbb{Q}}\mathbb{Q}_v$ is a division algebra exactly when $q'\in v$. Let $\Lambda_1\subset\mathbb{H}[\mathbb{Q},a,b]$ be a maximal order (an order not properly contained in any order), and let $\theta_1$ be an injective ring homomorphism from the subring `rationalEndSubring` $\kappa\,X_1$ (generated by the rationally represented endomorphisms) onto $\Lambda_1$ as a set. Let $\pi$ be a unit of $\mathbb{H}[\mathbb{Q},a,b]\otimes_{\mathbb{Q}}\mathbb{A}_{\mathbb{Q}}^{\mathrm{fin}}$ whose component at every prime $w$ with $q'\notin w$ is $1$, and whose associated lattice satisfies $\mathbb{H}\cap\pi\widehat{\Lambda_1}=\{h\in\Lambda_1: h=0\ \text{or}\ v_{q'}(\mathrm{nrd}\,h)\ge 1\}$. Let $x$ be a further such unit, $W/\kappa$ an elliptic curve, $\chi:X_1\to W$ a nonzero additive map on points lying in `rationalHomSet` (i.e. zero or rationally represented), and $d\in\mathbb{H}[\mathbb{Q},a,b]^{\times}$, and suppose $\theta_1$ maps the kernel ideal set $\{\rho\circ\chi:\rho\in\mathrm{rationalHomSet}(W,X_1)\}$ onto the image under quaternion conjugation of $d\cdot(\mathbb{H}\cap x\widehat{\Lambda_1})$. Then the same holds for the Frobenius-twisted map, $\chi$ followed by the $q'$-power Frobenius map $W\to W^{(q')}=W.\mathrm{map}(\mathrm{frobenius}\,\kappa\,q')$, with $x$ replaced by $x\pi$: the $\theta_1$-image of $\{\rho\circ(\mathrm{Frob}\circ\chi):\rho\in\mathrm{rationalHomSet}(W^{(q')},X_1)\}$ is the conjugate of $d\cdot(\mathbb{H}\cap x\pi\widehat{\Lambda_1})$.
--
--   This is the step of Deuring's theory, in the form used by Waterhouse, which says that under the correspondence between supersingular curves isogenous to $X_1$ and left ideal classes of $\mathrm{End}(X_1)$, composing with the $q'$-power Frobenius multiplies the kernel ideal of an isogeny by the two-sided prime $\mathfrak P$ of the maximal order above $q'$. It is used in the Čerednik–Drinfeld part of the argument to identify the Frobenius action on the supersingular points with a Hecke matrix on the quaternionic class set.
